{align:justify|In the autumn of the Mouse year, under the Kangxi Emperor of the Qing dynasty, the family of Truong Nguyen in Beijing bought a donkey. It was most vicious by nature — fond not only of kicking but of biting people too. Only the three members of Truong Nguyen's own family could ride it; whenever they mounted, it behaved most meekly and tame.}
{align:justify|But once, a Mr. Duong came to borrow the donkey and try riding it. Strangely, the donkey docilely let Mr. Duong ride away.}
{align:justify|That night Mr. Duong dreamed he saw a man in black saying:}
{align:justify|"I am the donkey in Truong Nguyen's house. In a past life I borrowed three hundred coins from you and never repaid them, so in this life I must repay you. Yesterday you rode me 280 miles; tomorrow morning, please ride me 20 miles more, and the debt I owe you will be settled."}
{align:justify|Mr. Duong asked: "Then how much do you owe the Truong family?"}
{align:justify|The man in black looked sorrowful and said:}
{align:justify|"Much, very much — beyond counting!"}
{align:justify|Waking, Mr. Duong went to the Truong house to borrow the donkey and rode a long stretch — when suddenly the donkey bucked and threw him off. Mr. Duong reckoned it up: he had ridden exactly 20 miles. Finding the affair truly marvelous, and the donkey most remarkable, Mr. Duong told it:}
{align:justify|"Now I understand and believe why you let me ride. But this place is ten miles from my home — if you won't carry me, how shall I get back? Let us agree: I shall ride you home, then spend ten strings of cash on hay for you. Agreed?"}
{align:justify|The donkey stood there gazing at Mr. Duong a long while, then consented to carry him home.}
{align:justify|(From the Buddhist Grace Gazette, volume 4)}
